1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,160
Aarav watches a pump fill the
overhead water tank in spurts.

2
00:00:04,510 --> 00:00:06,259
Spurt, gap, spurt, gap.

3
00:00:06,259 --> 00:00:08,812
Yet the tap below flows smooth and steady.

4
00:00:09,162 --> 00:00:12,766
The tank stores water in the spurts
and gives it out in the gaps.

5
00:00:13,116 --> 00:00:15,768
Our rectifier gives spurts too, the humps.

6
00:00:15,768 --> 00:00:17,098
Can we smooth them?

7
00:00:17,698 --> 00:00:19,602
First, what is wrong with humps?

8
00:00:19,952 --> 00:00:22,864
A phone or a radio wants
steady DC, a flat line.

9
00:00:23,214 --> 00:00:25,133
Humps keep rising and falling.

10
00:00:25,133 --> 00:00:27,004
That wobble is called ripple.

11
00:00:27,354 --> 00:00:30,335
Ripple is the leftover AC
riding on top of the DC.

12
00:00:30,935 --> 00:00:33,311
A circuit that removes
ripple is called a filter.

13
00:00:33,661 --> 00:00:37,838
The simplest filter is one
capacitor, placed across the load.

14
00:00:38,188 --> 00:00:41,962
A capacitor stores charge, just
like the tank stores water.

15
00:00:42,612 --> 00:00:46,531
Let's add one to our full-wave
rectifier on the breadboard.

16
00:00:46,881 --> 00:00:50,815
The capacitor C sits in
parallel with the load, RL.

17
00:00:51,165 --> 00:00:55,108
As the input rises, the diodes
conduct and charge the capacitor.

18
00:00:55,458 --> 00:00:58,097
It fills up to the peak, V m.

19
00:00:58,447 --> 00:01:01,849
Now the input falls below
the capacitor voltage.

20
00:01:02,199 --> 00:01:03,558
The diodes switch off.

21
00:01:03,558 --> 00:01:05,011
The supply is cut away.

22
00:01:05,361 --> 00:01:07,923
But the capacitor keeps feeding the load.

23
00:01:07,923 --> 00:01:09,128
It slowly drains.

24
00:01:09,478 --> 00:01:11,791
Then the next hump arrives
and tops it up again.

25
00:01:12,142 --> 00:01:15,903
So the output is nearly flat,
with a small sawtooth ripple.

26
00:01:16,553 --> 00:01:18,688
Now swap in a bigger capacitor.

27
00:01:18,688 --> 00:01:20,358
Watch the ripple shrink.

28
00:01:21,008 --> 00:01:25,356
A filter capacitor fills at the
peaks and feeds the load in the gaps.

29
00:01:26,156 --> 00:01:27,720
How big is the ripple?

30
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:29,532
Let's find it step by step.

31
00:01:29,882 --> 00:01:33,412
Between humps, the load
current I drains the capacitor.

32
00:01:34,112 --> 00:01:36,878
Charge lost equals current times time.

33
00:01:36,878 --> 00:01:38,927
Delta Q equals I times T.

34
00:01:39,627 --> 00:01:41,999
Here T is the gap between humps.

35
00:01:41,999 --> 00:01:44,257
For full-wave, one over two f.

36
00:01:44,957 --> 00:01:47,934
A capacitor's voltage drop
is charge over capacitance.

37
00:01:48,634 --> 00:01:52,235
So the ripple, V r, equals I over two f C.

38
00:01:52,935 --> 00:01:55,799
The current I is about V m over RL.

39
00:01:56,499 --> 00:01:59,863
So V r equals V m over two f RLC.

40
00:02:00,513 --> 00:02:03,057
For half-wave, the gap is twice as long.

41
00:02:03,407 --> 00:02:06,502
So V r equals V m over f RLC.

42
00:02:07,152 --> 00:02:08,646
Three ideas exams love.

43
00:02:08,996 --> 00:02:09,896
One.

44
00:02:09,519 --> 00:02:12,433
Bigger C or bigger RL means less ripple.

45
00:02:12,783 --> 00:02:16,253
The time RLC must be
much longer than the gap.

46
00:02:16,853 --> 00:02:17,753
Two.

47
00:02:17,325 --> 00:02:21,220
For the same parts, full-wave
ripple is half the half-wave ripple.

48
00:02:21,570 --> 00:02:23,569
Its gaps are only half as long.

49
00:02:24,168 --> 00:02:25,068
Three.

50
00:02:24,656 --> 00:02:27,243
Ripple factor compares
the leftover AC to the DC.

51
00:02:27,593 --> 00:02:31,948
Without a filter, half-wave has a
ripple factor of one point two one.

52
00:02:32,298 --> 00:02:34,422
Full-wave has zero point four eight.

53
00:02:34,422 --> 00:02:36,601
A good filter makes it far smaller.

54
00:02:37,251 --> 00:02:38,319
Worked example.

55
00:02:38,319 --> 00:02:42,095
A full-wave rectifier gives a
twenty volt peak at fifty hertz.

56
00:02:42,445 --> 00:02:44,064
The load is one kilo-ohm.

57
00:02:44,064 --> 00:02:46,572
The capacitor is one thousand microfarads.

58
00:02:46,922 --> 00:02:49,991
The humps come at two
f, one hundred hertz.

59
00:02:50,591 --> 00:02:52,789
Multiply two f, RL and C.

60
00:02:52,789 --> 00:02:54,531
That gives one hundred.

61
00:02:55,131 --> 00:02:57,775
So V r is twenty over one hundred.

62
00:02:57,775 --> 00:02:59,787
Just zero point two volts.

63
00:03:00,387 --> 00:03:04,080
The DC sits near the peak,
about nineteen point nine volts.

64
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:08,120
Without the filter, it was
only twelve point seven volts.

65
00:03:08,770 --> 00:03:10,231
Now, the exam trap.

66
00:03:10,581 --> 00:03:15,121
Many students put f equals fifty hertz
into the full-wave ripple formula.

67
00:03:15,471 --> 00:03:16,371
Wrong.

68
00:03:16,068 --> 00:03:18,083
Full-wave humps come at two f.

69
00:03:18,083 --> 00:03:19,639
Use one hundred hertz.

70
00:03:20,289 --> 00:03:21,557
Where is this asked?

71
00:03:21,557 --> 00:03:23,132
PUC and CBSE board papers.

72
00:03:23,482 --> 00:03:27,578
JEE Main twenty twenty one asked how
to get steady DC from a rectifier.

73
00:03:27,928 --> 00:03:31,595
Expect to draw the filtered
output, or find the ripple.

74
00:03:32,245 --> 00:03:33,145
Try this.

75
00:03:33,059 --> 00:03:37,042
A full-wave rectifier gives a
fifteen volt peak at fifty hertz.

76
00:03:37,392 --> 00:03:41,086
The load is five hundred ohms,
with two thousand microfarads.

77
00:03:41,086 --> 00:03:42,222
Find the ripple.

78
00:03:47,822 --> 00:03:48,722
B.

79
00:03:48,244 --> 00:03:50,514
About zero point one five volts.

80
00:03:50,864 --> 00:03:54,543
The ripple frequency is two
f, so one hundred hertz.

81
00:03:54,893 --> 00:04:00,193
One hundred times five hundred times
zero point zero zero two is one hundred.

82
00:04:00,543 --> 00:04:03,307
So V r is fifteen over one hundred.

83
00:04:03,307 --> 00:04:05,368
Zero point one five volts.

84
00:04:06,018 --> 00:04:07,564
Screenshot this card.

85
00:04:10,764 --> 00:04:12,283
Next, the Zener diode.

86
00:04:12,283 --> 00:04:14,414
How does it hold a voltage steady?
